icing of its  trick that's trying to control the  entire narrative of not only the film  that you've made but the marketing and  the critical cycle of it and there's  only so much control you can exert and  obviously they want as much control as  they can get  but the degrees that they're having to  go do to try and keep this cycle going I  don't know if it's worth it anymore  but I can look at something like say  endgame and this is not me trying to  aggrandize Disney and Marvel especially  when it comes to a marketing and  theatrical release business practices  Disney in particular is actually kind of  awful but specifically in terms of the  end result and the coming back to the  artistic element and the execution of  the product itself I can look at  something like endgame and the fact that  Thanos dies within like 10 minutes of  the movie and I didn't see that coming I  don't know anyone who saw that coming  and that could be considered a massive  massive twist but it's also something  that you never would have guessed given  the marketing there wasn't any publicity  around this idea of oh wait do you see  what we do with their nose specifically  they didn't build that up the way that  there was this built up mystery around  whatever the heck fake  name they gave benedict cumberbatch is  con for star trek into darkness or  Blofeld for spectre or ray for star wars  although ray is complicated because that  is made more difficult by the fact that  they very clearly didn't really have a  plan except to build mystery that was  the plan I don't feel like the decision  to kill thanos early in that film was  made because it would feed a marketing  cycle that feels like a decision that  was made so that they could tell a more  interesting story and that's the thing  surprises are good for marketing but if  you don't have one organically trying to  force one in or build a surprise around  a character that otherwise has no need  to have a twist you're just gonna end up  w